# 1Password (scoped tokens per repo)

- Use the Linux `op` CLI with a service-account token, scoped per profile to its own vaults. A repo's
  `.claude/settings.local.json` overrides the global one:

  | Profile | Vaults | Settings file |
  |---|---|---|
  | `global` | `zgeoff` (rw) | `~/.claude/settings.json` |
  | `vers` | `vers` (rw), `vers-ci` (r) | `~/projects/vers/.claude/settings.local.json` |
  | `cloud` | `cloud` (rw) | `~/projects/cloud/.claude/settings.local.json` |

  `vers-ci` holds only what the vers infra-drift CI job reads. `zgeoff` holds the items every agent
  shares, such as `repo-sync-github-app` and the `zgeoff-agent` login, token and SSH key. `cloud` holds
  the zgeoff/cloud infra secrets. My personal vaults are reachable only through `op-win`, with my approval.
- Claude Code loads the token from `env.OP_SERVICE_ACCOUNT_TOKEN`, so plain `op item get …` works
  there. Any other agent (or a Claude session started before a re-mint) reads it inline:
  `OP_SERVICE_ACCOUNT_TOKEN=$(jq -r .env.OP_SERVICE_ACCOUNT_TOKEN <settings file>) op …`.
- Tokens expire ~7d. When an `op` call fails auth, re-mint with `op-mint <profile>` (dotfiles
  `~/.local/bin/op-mint`; `op-mint --list` shows the profiles). Don't ask me to sign in the Linux CLI;
  `op-mint` mints through `op-win`, which reaches my Windows 1Password app; approving may need a tap.
  A new profile or vault means editing `op-mint`, not minting by hand.
- Never echo a token.
- Each mint makes a new `claude-<profile>-<date>` service account — prune stale ones in the
  1Password web UI occasionally.

