# GitHub identity

- Agents act on GitHub as the machine user `zgeoff-agent`: a Write collaborator with no admin rights
  and no ruleset bypass. `GH_TOKEN`, `GITHUB_TOKEN` and `GIT_SSH_COMMAND` in Claude's settings point at it.
- Every zgeoff repo has the `main protection` ruleset: a direct push to `main` fails by design, so open
  a PR. atc and vers need my approving review.
- Never switch to my own login to get past a rule: no `env -u GH_TOKEN`, `gh auth switch`,
  `~/.ssh/id_ed25519` or `gh pr merge --admin`. Ask me instead.
- New repo: `agent-onboard <repo> [--review]` grants the bot access and applies the ruleset. New machine:
  `agent-identity` writes the bot's key and token from 1Password.

# Stack taste

- Pro: Bun, Elysia, Kysely, TanStack (Start/Query). Typed API layers (oRPC-class) — never hand-rolled REST.
- Anti: Next.js, Remix / React Router framework mode, Drizzle.
- TypeScript 7 / tsgo: adopt eagerly, RCs included — don't propose "safe" holdbacks.
- Personal projects: interesting > safe; experimental APIs welcome.

# Building and reviewing

- Medium or larger work runs through the `build-feature` workflow: anything that needs a written plan, adds behaviour, or touches more than a few files. Agree the plan first, then load the `build-feature` skill and run the workflow. Small fixes and doc changes don't need it.
- Every PR gets at least one local review before hand-off: Codex first, or an Opus review agent when Codex is unavailable or times out. Answer every finding: fix it, or reply with why not.
- CodeRabbit: check for its review and answer its findings. If it is rate-limited, paused, or hasn't reviewed within 10 minutes of the PR opening, move on. It never blocks a PR that has a local review.
